# Slime: how much borax is enough?
#
# White glue is long chains of poly(vinyl alcohol) drifting past each other,
# which is why it pours. Borax puts borate bridges between the chains. The
# bridges keep breaking and re-forming, so the result is neither liquid nor
# solid: it flows if you wait and tears if you pull. Nothing is used up —
# the borate is a linker, not a reagent — so the question is not "does it
# react" but "how much is enough".
register lv1

# Too little. The chains are barely joined and it still runs.
add v1 water 100mL
add v1 pva_glue 50mL
add v1 borax 0.05g
stir v1
inspect v1

# The classroom amount. Most of the polymer is now in the network.
new beaker
add v2 water 100mL
add v2 pva_glue 50mL
add v2 borax 0.25g
stir v2
inspect v2

# And well past it. Beyond saturation more borax buys nothing: every chain
# that can be bridged already is, which is why over-boraxed slime goes
# rubbery rather than more slimy.
new beaker
add v3 water 100mL
add v3 pva_glue 50mL
add v3 borax 2g
stir v3
inspect v3

# Read the ledger in any of them: the borax is still there, all of it. A
# crosslinker is not consumed, and that is the difference between this and
# every other reaction in this lab.

# What this does not model: the dose response is a bounded teaching curve
# calibrated to the familiar 50 mL-glue ratio, not measured rheology — no
# modulus, no relaxation time, no stringiness. Whether a particular glue
# gels at all depends on its degree of hydrolysis, and poly(vinyl acetate)
# glues do not gel this way at all. Household borax is the decahydrate, so
# a little over half of what the box weighs is the salt doing the work.
